sockmap: a source is paused while its redirect target's backlog is deep

A redirect consumes the source's data the moment the verdict runs: TCP's
copied_seq advances (tcp_eat_skb) and the window stays open, vsock's credit
is returned in read_skb, while the bytes sit in the target's ingress_skb
backlog waiting for its worker to send them. Nothing bounds that queue. A
container sending a bulk stream to the host (iperf3 through the joined
sockets) filled the guest's memory in a second and the OOM killer took
dockerd.

Now the target's backlog is counted, and when it passes a high mark every
source redirecting into it is paused: its data-ready callback returns
without reading, so data accumulates in its own receive queue and the
protocol's own flow control reaches the sender — TCP's window closes,
vsock's credit runs out and the host stops reading its socket. When the
backlog drains below a low mark the sources are resumed from their own
backlog workers, the one context that may take a socket's lock, and the
read that follows drains the receive queue in order, marking EOF, sending
TCP's window update. One global lock over the pause lists lets a source and
its target be torn down in either order.

And the wake after EAGAIN: the backlog parks a jiffy when the target has no
room, and the write-space callback's schedule deferred to that timer, so a
stream into a socket that runs out of room by design (vsock, on credit)
moved one window per tick — 4.3 Gbit/s at HZ=250. The callback now pulls
the timer forward.

